# DiscordianAI log rotation fragment.
#
# Install it as /etc/logrotate.d/discordianai and replace the placeholders
# below: the bot.log path, and USER/GROUP with the account and group that own
# the log directory. The su directive is required when the log's parent
# directory is world-writable or group-writable by a group other than root,
# because logrotate otherwise refuses with "parent directory has insecure
# permissions"; it tells logrotate which account to rotate as.
#
# Rotation keeps history rather than trimming it. Rotated files are
# date-stamped, `delaycompress` leaves the newest one uncompressed, and older
# generations are compressed. The generation count is high enough that the
# archive is effectively unbounded at this bot's volume: at roughly 50 MB
# every few months, 50 rotated files of at least 100 MB each is about 5 GB of
# archives, plus the live file, which is at least 100 MB plus the growth since
# the previous check (about one day's growth with a daily check). The size is
# only checked when logrotate runs, so both figures are lower bounds. That is
# years of logs. Compressed files are smaller. If you ever need a hard limit, lower
# `rotate` — that is the only knob that deletes anything.
#
# copytruncate is required because the bot holds bot.log open through the
# supervisor's append redirection and cannot be asked to reopen it. It copies
# the file and then truncates it in place, so a line written in the moment
# between the copy and the truncate can be lost. The alternative — routing
# output through journald and rotating that instead — removes the window but
# moves the logs out of bot.log entirely.

/path/to/DiscordianAI/bot.log {
    su USER GROUP
    size 100M
    rotate 50
    compress
    delaycompress
    dateext
    dateformat -%Y-%m-%d
    missingok
    notifempty
    copytruncate
}
